        "idea": "Direct constrained NLP via scipy.optimize.minimize (SLSQP) on the reformulated problem: maximize t subject to d(i,j)^2 >= t and d(i,j)^2 <= 1",
        "description": "The evaluator hints the exact reformulation: introduce auxiliary variable t and solve max t with 91 lower-bound constraints d(i,j)^2 >= t and 91 upper-bound constraints d(i,j)^2 <= 1 over 42+1 variables. Implement as scipy.optimize.minimize with method='SLSQP' minimizing -t, passing all constraints as a single vectorized inequality function returning np.concatenate([d2 - t, 1 - d2]). Use analytic gradients: for constraint d(i,j)^2, gradient wrt point i is 2(xi-xj). Run from many seeds (the current best configuration plus icosahedron-based and random starts), and take the best feasible result. SLSQP handles this smooth problem well and converges to KKT points of the true ratio objective, unlike soft-min gradient heuristics which stall near saddle regions. Finally rescale so dmax=1.",
        "what_to_optimize": "primary combined_score (dmin/dmax)^2, converging to the true local/global optimum of the exact ratio",
        "cautions": "Scale points so dmax is near 1 before optimization; provide the Jacobian of the constraint vector (sparse-like dense array) for speed and reliability; check feasibility after solve and fall back to best seed if SLSQP fails; keep number of restarts modest to fit timeout.",
        "approach_type": "scipy.optimize.minimize"
      },
      {
